## Course overview

Visual Arts at Standard Level treats art as both **making and thinking**. Students develop a personal studio practice while learning to research, contextualise, and curate their work. The course moves fluidly between three interlocking areas: *visual arts in context*, *visual arts methods*, and *communicating visual arts*.

## What you will study

- **Studio practice** — drawing, painting, printmaking, sculpture, photography, or digital media.
- **Art history & theory** — investigating artists, movements, and cultural contexts.
- **Curatorial practice** — selecting, framing, and presenting work to an audience.

## Assessment

| Component | Weighting | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Comparative Study | 20% | Analysis of artworks from different contexts |
| Process Portfolio | 40% | Evidence of sustained studio experimentation |
| Exhibition | 40% | A curated selection of resolved artworks |

## The Exhibition

The final **Exhibition** is the heart of the course: a curated body of resolved work, accompanied by a curatorial rationale that explains your intentions and the relationships between pieces. It is shown publicly at the school each year.
